1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:04,300
Every SAT math question
lets you use Desmos,

2
00:00:04,320 --> 00:00:07,300
the graphing calculator
built right into the test.

3
00:00:07,320 --> 00:00:10,459
A lot of students only
use it for arithmetic.

4
00:00:10,479 --> 00:00:13,290
That leaves a lot of
easy points on the table.

5
00:00:13,820 --> 00:00:18,840
Here are eleven ways to use
it on real SAT question types,

6
00:00:18,860 --> 00:00:21,630
starting basic and getting more clever.

7
00:00:21,859 --> 00:00:25,030
Each one comes with a
practice question you can try.

8
00:00:25,506 --> 00:00:28,526
And at the end, I'll show
you when not to use Desmos,

9
00:00:28,546 --> 00:00:31,436
because sometimes it's slower
than just doing the math.

10
00:00:31,943 --> 00:00:32,923
Trick one.

11
00:00:32,943 --> 00:00:35,483
Solve any equation by graphing.

12
00:00:35,503 --> 00:00:41,203
Here's a question: What value of x
satisfies three times x minus four,

13
00:00:41,223 --> 00:00:44,633
plus seven, equals two x plus nine?

14
00:00:45,053 --> 00:00:49,142
Pause and try it, with or without Desmos.

15
00:00:53,965 --> 00:01:00,105
In Desmos, type the left side as
y equals three times x minus four,

16
00:01:00,125 --> 00:01:01,295
plus seven.

17
00:01:01,485 --> 00:01:05,825
Then type the right side as
y equals two x plus nine.

18
00:01:05,845 --> 00:01:06,985
Two lines.

19
00:01:07,005 --> 00:01:09,454
The answer is wherever they cross.

20
00:01:09,965 --> 00:01:13,665
You'll need to zoom out to see it,
because they cross pretty far from the

21
00:01:13,685 --> 00:01:14,685
middle.

22
00:01:14,816 --> 00:01:18,555
Click the intersection, and
Desmos shows the point fourteen,

23
00:01:18,575 --> 00:01:19,556
thirty-seven.

24
00:01:19,576 --> 00:01:21,756
The x value is the answer.

25
00:01:21,776 --> 00:01:23,705
x equals fourteen.

26
00:01:24,161 --> 00:01:27,460
You can also type the whole
equation exactly as written,

27
00:01:27,480 --> 00:01:31,261
and Desmos draws a vertical
line at the solution.

28
00:01:31,281 --> 00:01:35,570
Either way, you never had to
distribute or move a single term.

29
00:01:36,036 --> 00:01:37,086
Trick two.

30
00:01:37,316 --> 00:01:39,216
Systems of equations.

31
00:01:39,236 --> 00:01:42,366
If x and y is the solution to the system,

32
00:01:42,396 --> 00:01:47,886
two x plus three y equals
twelve, and x minus y equals one,

33
00:01:47,996 --> 00:01:50,406
what is x plus y?

34
00:01:50,864 --> 00:01:52,274
Pause and try it.

35
00:01:57,073 --> 00:02:01,173
Type both equations into Desmos
exactly as they're written.

36
00:02:01,193 --> 00:02:03,693
You don't need to solve for y first.

37
00:02:03,713 --> 00:02:06,843
Desmos handles that form just fine.

38
00:02:07,293 --> 00:02:10,713
Click where they cross: three, two.

39
00:02:10,733 --> 00:02:14,113
So x is three and y is two.

40
00:02:14,133 --> 00:02:15,633
But read the question again.

41
00:02:15,653 --> 00:02:17,592
It asks for x plus y.

42
00:02:17,612 --> 00:02:20,663
The answer is five, not three.

43
00:02:21,092 --> 00:02:22,512
Trick three.

44
00:02:22,532 --> 00:02:24,192
Counting solutions.

45
00:02:24,212 --> 00:02:27,662
How many solutions does this system have?

46
00:02:28,092 --> 00:02:31,711
y equals x squared minus four x plus one,

47
00:02:31,731 --> 00:02:35,182
and y equals negative two x plus one.

48
00:02:35,590 --> 00:02:36,000
Pause and try it.

49
00:02:41,799 --> 00:02:43,179
Graph both.

50
00:02:43,199 --> 00:02:46,499
Every place the graphs
cross is one solution.

51
00:02:46,519 --> 00:02:50,139
Here, the line cuts through
the parabola in two places.

52
00:02:50,159 --> 00:02:51,659
So there are two solutions.

53
00:02:51,679 --> 00:02:53,048
The answer is C.

54
00:02:53,493 --> 00:02:54,873
Same idea for lines.

55
00:02:54,893 --> 00:02:57,352
If two lines cross once, one solution.

56
00:02:57,372 --> 00:02:59,593
If they're parallel, no solution.

57
00:02:59,613 --> 00:03:01,952
If they land right on top of each other,

58
00:03:01,972 --> 00:03:03,382
infinitely many.

59
00:03:03,834 --> 00:03:05,014
Trick four.

60
00:03:05,034 --> 00:03:08,054
X-intercepts, also called zeros, roots,

61
00:03:08,074 --> 00:03:09,334
or solutions.

62
00:03:09,354 --> 00:03:14,414
What is the sum of the solutions to x
squared minus seven x plus ten equals

63
00:03:14,434 --> 00:03:15,434
zero?

64
00:03:15,675 --> 00:03:17,085
Pause and try it.

65
00:03:21,884 --> 00:03:26,424
Graph y equals x squared
minus seven x plus ten,

66
00:03:26,444 --> 00:03:28,414
and click the curve.

67
00:03:28,524 --> 00:03:31,544
Desmos marks where it crosses the x axis:

68
00:03:31,564 --> 00:03:33,304
two and five.

69
00:03:33,324 --> 00:03:36,094
Add them up, and the answer is seven.

70
00:03:36,530 --> 00:03:39,630
Whenever a question says zeros, roots,

71
00:03:39,650 --> 00:03:42,790
x-intercepts, or solutions of a quadratic,

72
00:03:42,810 --> 00:03:46,900
they all mean the same thing:
where the graph hits the x axis.

73
00:03:47,480 --> 00:03:48,620
Trick five.

74
00:03:48,640 --> 00:03:50,850
Maximums and minimums.

75
00:03:51,040 --> 00:03:54,700
The height of a ball, in feet,
t seconds after it's launched,

76
00:03:54,720 --> 00:04:00,820
is h of t equals negative sixteen t
squared plus sixty-four t plus five.

77
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:02,369
What's the maximum height?

78
00:04:02,865 --> 00:04:04,275
Pause and try it.

79
00:04:09,074 --> 00:04:13,724
Desmos graphs with x and y, so
type it with x instead of t:

80
00:04:13,794 --> 00:04:19,734
y equals negative sixteen x squared
plus sixty-four x plus five.

81
00:04:19,754 --> 00:04:22,254
Zoom out, because the graph goes way up.

82
00:04:22,274 --> 00:04:24,453
Then click the top of the curve.

83
00:04:24,473 --> 00:04:27,244
Desmos shows two, sixty-nine.

84
00:04:27,797 --> 00:04:29,047
Now be careful.

85
00:04:29,157 --> 00:04:31,656
Two is the time when the ball is highest.

86
00:04:31,676 --> 00:04:33,647
Sixty-nine is the height.

87
00:04:33,837 --> 00:04:37,927
The question asks for height,
so the answer is sixty-nine.

88
00:04:38,443 --> 00:04:39,693
Trick six.

89
00:04:39,843 --> 00:04:41,253
Sliders.

90
00:04:41,643 --> 00:04:45,893
This one handles those questions
with a mystery constant.

91
00:04:46,203 --> 00:04:49,863
In the system k x plus three y equals six,

92
00:04:49,883 --> 00:04:54,062
and two x plus y equals
five, k is a constant.

93
00:04:54,082 --> 00:04:58,093
If the system has no solution, what is k?

94
00:04:58,584 --> 00:04:59,994
Pause and try it.

95
00:05:04,793 --> 00:05:07,603
Type both equations into Desmos.

96
00:05:07,873 --> 00:05:12,693
Because k isn't defined, Desmos
offers to add a slider for it.

97
00:05:12,713 --> 00:05:13,713
Click it.

98
00:05:13,878 --> 00:05:17,018
No solution means the lines
are parallel and never cross.

99
00:05:17,038 --> 00:05:20,688
So drag k until the lines are parallel.

100
00:05:20,758 --> 00:05:23,978
Tip: set the slider to
move in steps of one,

101
00:05:23,998 --> 00:05:25,857
so it lands on whole numbers.

102
00:05:25,877 --> 00:05:29,048
The lines go parallel at k equals six.

103
00:05:29,578 --> 00:05:35,318
To double check, the slope of two x
plus y equals five is negative two.

104
00:05:35,338 --> 00:05:41,637
The slope of k x plus three y
equals six is negative k over three.

105
00:05:41,657 --> 00:05:44,628
Set them equal, and k is six.

106
00:05:45,378 --> 00:05:48,358
Sliders also work for questions like,

107
00:05:48,378 --> 00:05:54,388
for what value of c does this parabola
touch the x axis exactly once?

108
00:05:54,838 --> 00:05:56,048
Trick seven.

109
00:05:56,078 --> 00:05:57,447
Inequalities.

110
00:05:57,557 --> 00:06:04,217
Which point is a solution to the system
y is greater than two x minus three,

111
00:06:04,237 --> 00:06:08,688
and y is less than or equal
to negative x plus six?

112
00:06:09,164 --> 00:06:10,574
Pause and try it.

113
00:06:15,373 --> 00:06:17,423
Type both inequalities.

114
00:06:17,853 --> 00:06:23,503
Desmos shades each one, and the solutions
are wherever the shading overlaps.

115
00:06:23,612 --> 00:06:27,752
The dashed line means
greater than, not equal to,

116
00:06:27,772 --> 00:06:30,063
so points on that line don't count.

117
00:06:30,480 --> 00:06:33,170
Now type the four points from the choices.

118
00:06:33,279 --> 00:06:36,900
Only one lands inside the overlap: one,

119
00:06:36,920 --> 00:06:37,620
two.

120
00:06:37,640 --> 00:06:39,369
The answer is C.

121
00:06:39,791 --> 00:06:41,041
Trick eight.

122
00:06:41,191 --> 00:06:42,360
Regression.

123
00:06:42,510 --> 00:06:46,091
This is the most underrated Desmos move.

124
00:06:46,111 --> 00:06:49,451
The table shows three points
from a linear relationship:

125
00:06:49,471 --> 00:06:54,721
one and seven, three and
thirteen, six and twenty-two.

126
00:06:54,911 --> 00:06:56,721
Which equation represents it?

127
00:06:57,125 --> 00:06:58,535
Pause and try it.

128
00:07:03,334 --> 00:07:06,863
In Desmos, add a table
and type the points in.

129
00:07:07,053 --> 00:07:11,313
Then, on a new line, type y
one, then the tilde symbol,

130
00:07:11,333 --> 00:07:15,143
the little squiggle, then m x one plus b.

131
00:07:15,774 --> 00:07:18,823
Desmos finds the line
that fits the points.

132
00:07:19,270 --> 00:07:22,560
It gives m equals three and b equals four.

133
00:07:22,669 --> 00:07:25,730
So the answer is y
equals three x plus four,

134
00:07:25,750 --> 00:07:26,890
choice A.

135
00:07:26,910 --> 00:07:31,050
Notice B and C both work for the
first point but not the others.

136
00:07:31,070 --> 00:07:34,400
That's exactly the trap
regression protects you from.

137
00:07:34,812 --> 00:07:41,222
For exponential tables, use y
one tilde a times b to the x one.

138
00:07:41,252 --> 00:07:42,422
Same idea.

139
00:07:42,885 --> 00:07:43,745
Trick nine.

140
00:07:43,765 --> 00:07:45,385
Equivalent expressions.

141
00:07:45,405 --> 00:07:48,305
Which expression is
equivalent to x plus three,

142
00:07:48,325 --> 00:07:51,495
squared, minus two times x plus three?

143
00:07:52,024 --> 00:07:53,434
Pause and try it.

144
00:07:58,233 --> 00:08:01,213
Graph the original expression as y equals.

145
00:08:01,233 --> 00:08:04,123
Then graph the choices one at a time.

146
00:08:04,233 --> 00:08:07,212
Equivalent expressions
make the exact same graph,

147
00:08:07,232 --> 00:08:10,843
so the right answer will sit
perfectly on top of the original.

148
00:08:11,307 --> 00:08:13,686
Choice A lands right on top.

149
00:08:13,706 --> 00:08:18,237
The answer is x squared
plus four x plus three.

150
00:08:18,664 --> 00:08:19,754
Trick ten.

151
00:08:19,864 --> 00:08:21,074
Functions.

152
00:08:21,304 --> 00:08:25,444
If f of x equals two
x squared minus three,

153
00:08:25,464 --> 00:08:31,473
and g of x equals x plus four,
what is f of g of negative one?

154
00:08:31,957 --> 00:08:33,367
Pause and try it.

155
00:08:38,166 --> 00:08:41,936
Desmos understands function notation.

156
00:08:42,166 --> 00:08:46,826
Type f of x equals two
x squared minus three.

157
00:08:46,846 --> 00:08:50,225
Type g of x equals x plus four.

158
00:08:50,245 --> 00:08:55,536
Then type f of g of negative
one, and Desmos just tells you:

159
00:08:55,566 --> 00:08:56,566
fifteen.

160
00:08:56,999 --> 00:09:02,779
By hand, g of negative one is three,
and f of three is eighteen minus three,

161
00:09:02,799 --> 00:09:04,329
which is fifteen.

162
00:09:04,599 --> 00:09:09,808
Same answer, but Desmos makes it
nearly impossible to slip on the signs.

163
00:09:10,264 --> 00:09:11,314
Trick eleven.

164
00:09:11,504 --> 00:09:12,873
Statistics.

165
00:09:13,143 --> 00:09:17,124
For the data set four, eight, eight, ten,

166
00:09:17,144 --> 00:09:21,673
fifteen, twenty-one, what is
the mean minus the median?

167
00:09:22,139 --> 00:09:23,549
Pause and try it.

168
00:09:28,348 --> 00:09:33,448
Type L equals, then the
numbers inside square brackets.

169
00:09:33,468 --> 00:09:35,488
Now L is a list.

170
00:09:35,508 --> 00:09:41,038
Type mean of L minus median of
L, and Desmos gives you two.

171
00:09:41,499 --> 00:09:45,028
The mean is eleven and the median is nine.

172
00:09:45,099 --> 00:09:49,079
Desmos also has functions for
things like standard deviation,

173
00:09:49,099 --> 00:09:53,988
but on the SAT you'll usually just compare
spreads instead of calculating them.

174
00:09:54,458 --> 00:09:57,558
Now, when shouldn't you use Desmos?

175
00:09:57,578 --> 00:09:59,798
First, word problems.

176
00:09:59,818 --> 00:10:01,588
Desmos can't read.

177
00:10:01,738 --> 00:10:04,358
You still have to turn the
words into an equation,

178
00:10:04,378 --> 00:10:06,187
and that's usually the hard part.

179
00:10:06,622 --> 00:10:09,912
Second, quick arithmetic
and one-step algebra.

180
00:10:10,062 --> 00:10:13,442
If you can do it in your
head in five seconds,

181
00:10:13,462 --> 00:10:15,752
opening Desmos is slower.

182
00:10:16,225 --> 00:10:18,754
Third, watch the window.

183
00:10:18,785 --> 00:10:22,445
If you don't see an
intersection or a vertex,

184
00:10:22,465 --> 00:10:25,114
zoom out before you decide it isn't there.

185
00:10:25,555 --> 00:10:27,165
And one bonus tip.

186
00:10:27,235 --> 00:10:31,175
If the answer choices have
square roots or pi in them,

187
00:10:31,195 --> 00:10:35,055
type each choice into Desmos
to turn it into a decimal,

188
00:10:35,075 --> 00:10:37,325
then compare it to your answer.

189
00:10:37,768 --> 00:10:38,548
Quick recap.

190
00:10:38,568 --> 00:10:40,667
Solve equations by graphing.

191
00:10:40,687 --> 00:10:42,788
Click intersections for systems.

192
00:10:42,808 --> 00:10:44,108
Count crossings.

193
00:10:44,128 --> 00:10:46,147
Click intercepts and vertices.

194
00:10:46,167 --> 00:10:48,668
Use sliders for mystery constants.

195
00:10:48,688 --> 00:10:51,028
Test points against inequalities.

196
00:10:51,048 --> 00:10:52,987
Run regressions on tables.

197
00:10:53,007 --> 00:10:55,688
Graph expressions to check
if they're equivalent.

198
00:10:55,708 --> 00:10:57,248
Evaluate functions.

199
00:10:57,268 --> 00:11:00,158
And use lists for statistics.

200
00:11:00,602 --> 00:11:04,662
The best way to get fast is to practice
with the same calculator you'll use on

201
00:11:04,682 --> 00:11:05,732
test day.

202
00:11:05,962 --> 00:11:08,412
Bluebook's practice
tests have it built in.

203
00:11:08,858 --> 00:11:12,117
Every practice question here
was written for this video,

204
00:11:12,137 --> 00:11:14,878
in the style of the real test.

205
00:11:14,898 --> 00:11:16,988
If this helped, subscribe.

206
00:11:17,017 --> 00:11:23,798
The next video is ten hard SAT math
questions to try before the November seventh

207
00:11:23,818 --> 00:11:24,798
test.

208
00:11:24,818 --> 00:11:25,818
Good luck.
